WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.490 --> 00:00:05.478
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:41.380 --> 00:00:42.850
When we think about
trash, we often

00:00:42.850 --> 00:00:44.560
don't imagine the
garments that we wear.

00:00:44.560 --> 00:00:46.360
But when we're done
with them, they also

00:00:46.360 --> 00:00:48.100
end up in landfills,
just like the trash

00:00:48.100 --> 00:00:50.320
that we throw out every day.

00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:53.140
The fashion industry is the
second top waste-producing

00:00:53.140 --> 00:00:54.700
industry in the world.

00:00:54.700 --> 00:00:56.470
Our trend cycle moves
extremely quickly,

00:00:56.470 --> 00:00:59.560
which encourages consumers
to purchase and dispose

00:00:59.560 --> 00:01:02.500
at a rapid rate, but that
leads to a lot of damage

00:01:02.500 --> 00:01:06.660
to our environment from the
waste produced by our garments.

00:01:06.660 --> 00:01:09.250
The MIT Trashion Show is
an annual fashion show

00:01:09.250 --> 00:01:12.310
put on by MIT's Undergraduate
Association's Committee

00:01:12.310 --> 00:01:13.960
on Sustainability.

00:01:13.960 --> 00:01:16.090
And every year, we
invite designers

00:01:16.090 --> 00:01:17.680
from all over the
Boston community

00:01:17.680 --> 00:01:21.130
to design garments out of
unconventional or up-cycled

00:01:21.130 --> 00:01:24.220
materials, and then have them
shown at our fashion show.

00:01:24.220 --> 00:01:26.110
The show started 11
years ago, and we've

00:01:26.110 --> 00:01:27.410
come a long way since then.

00:01:27.410 --> 00:01:28.998
But the Trashion
Show's main goal

00:01:28.998 --> 00:01:30.790
remains the same as it
did when it started,

00:01:30.790 --> 00:01:34.000
which is to promote
awareness about the waste

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:36.190
that the fashion
industry produces,

00:01:36.190 --> 00:01:39.903
and spark conversation about
where our clothes come from,

00:01:39.903 --> 00:01:41.320
how they were made,
and where they

00:01:41.320 --> 00:01:44.320
go once we're done with them.

00:01:44.320 --> 00:01:49.480
Designers are encouraged to use
trash, or recycled materials,

00:01:49.480 --> 00:01:51.160
or materials that
would otherwise

00:01:51.160 --> 00:01:55.000
be thrown away, in order to
create pieces that can be worn.

00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:59.470
So past materials have included
newspapers, soda boxes,

00:01:59.470 --> 00:02:04.110
soda cans, electrical
wires, and things like that.

00:02:04.110 --> 00:02:06.230
Clothing is usually very flowy.

00:02:06.230 --> 00:02:08.610
It's very organic,
and needs to mold

00:02:08.610 --> 00:02:10.479
to the shape of a human body.

00:02:10.479 --> 00:02:16.940
So working around those material
properties can be difficult.

00:02:16.940 --> 00:02:19.520
Typically, the designer
that wants to participate

00:02:19.520 --> 00:02:21.800
is someone that wants
to tackle a challenge.

00:02:21.800 --> 00:02:23.780
They come from all majors
and all backgrounds,

00:02:23.780 --> 00:02:27.420
but ultimately, they're
looking for a creative outlet.

00:02:27.420 --> 00:02:31.080
It's not very easy to create
a garment out of trash,

00:02:31.080 --> 00:02:32.800
and it takes a lot of time.

00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:35.350
So these people are
driven by risk taking.

00:02:35.350 --> 00:02:38.930
And they want to make a product
that otherwise wouldn't exist.

00:02:42.170 --> 00:02:45.400
The Trashion Show actually
takes a lot of work to organize.

00:02:45.400 --> 00:02:47.590
You need to have AV.

00:02:47.590 --> 00:02:50.950
You need to talk to
Facilities, book a venue,

00:02:50.950 --> 00:02:54.400
and then also keep track of all
the models and designers who

00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:58.200
are participating in the show.

00:02:58.200 --> 00:03:00.360
Often, people are
just really amazed

00:03:00.360 --> 00:03:02.850
to see a product that
they had just thrown away

00:03:02.850 --> 00:03:07.200
worn on someone's body, and worn
in a way that is a functional

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:07.830
garment.

00:03:07.830 --> 00:03:09.990
That's something that
people don't usually

00:03:09.990 --> 00:03:13.290
get to see around campus.

00:03:13.290 --> 00:03:14.970
When it comes to
judging, we have

00:03:14.970 --> 00:03:18.930
a panel of three judges, all
related to sustainability,

00:03:18.930 --> 00:03:22.210
but also coming from
different disciplines.

00:03:22.210 --> 00:03:24.790
We provide them
a set of criteria

00:03:24.790 --> 00:03:28.420
that involves whether an
outfit is sustainable,

00:03:28.420 --> 00:03:31.390
how much recycled
material it uses,

00:03:31.390 --> 00:03:36.130
how aesthetically it works,
and the technical fabrication

00:03:36.130 --> 00:03:39.670
skills of the piece.

00:03:39.670 --> 00:03:42.210
So we give out a few
different awards at the show--

00:03:42.210 --> 00:03:44.880
first, second, and third,
and audience choice, but also

00:03:44.880 --> 00:03:47.310
other committees on the
Undergraduate Association

00:03:47.310 --> 00:03:50.190
Committee for Sustainability
give out prizes based off

00:03:50.190 --> 00:03:51.638
of what they do.

00:03:51.638 --> 00:03:53.430
So there's a lot of
different opportunities

00:03:53.430 --> 00:03:55.710
to celebrate the different
aspects of pieces

00:03:55.710 --> 00:03:56.660
that designers create.

00:04:00.250 --> 00:04:03.220
When people think of MIT, they
usually don't think of fashion.

00:04:03.220 --> 00:04:04.900
But in fact, a lot
of students here

00:04:04.900 --> 00:04:09.850
are very interested in
fashion or just creating art.

00:04:09.850 --> 00:04:11.890
And something really
special about MIT

00:04:11.890 --> 00:04:14.670
is that kids have a
wide array of interests.

00:04:14.670 --> 00:04:16.329
And maybe if fashion
isn't one of them,

00:04:16.329 --> 00:04:19.029
the Trashion Show encourages
them to try something new.

00:04:19.029 --> 00:04:21.700
And MIT kids always love
trying something new

00:04:21.700 --> 00:04:23.260
and pushing their boundaries.

00:04:23.260 --> 00:04:26.020
And creating clothes out
of trash is a challenge.

00:04:26.020 --> 00:04:29.515
And it takes engineering,
and design, and technology,

00:04:29.515 --> 00:04:31.140
which are all things
that students here

00:04:31.140 --> 00:04:34.060
are very interested in.

00:04:34.060 --> 00:04:37.110
[MUSIC PLAYING]

